WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.093
Last time, we rebuilt the post office.

2
00:00:05.093 --> 00:00:11.706
Instead of a clerk for every customer, each customer gets a box in the wall, with a small light above it.

3
00:00:11.706 --> 00:00:15.925
One clerk watches the wall, and serves only the boxes that are lit.

4
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:19.289
But here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:22.382
How does that one clerk know which boxes are lit?

6
00:00:22.382 --> 00:00:26.927
With ten thousand boxes, does the clerk walk past every one, every time?

7
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.924
That's how the old select and poll worked: read out every box number, one by one.

8
00:00:34.924 --> 00:00:38.741
What we want is a wall that hands the clerk a short list.

9
00:00:38.741 --> 00:00:39.545
These three.

10
00:00:39.545 --> 00:00:40.215
Right now.

11
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:42.579
In Java, that wall is a Selector.

12
00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:44.725
It watches many channels at once.

13
00:00:44.725 --> 00:00:49.733
A channel is Java's handle on something bytes move through, such as a socket.

14
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:51.553
And the clerk is one thread.

15
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:59.022
You hang a box on the wall by registering a channel, with an interest set: the lights this box may show.

16
00:00:59.022 --> 00:01:02.426
OP_ACCEPT means a new customer is waiting at the door.

17
00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:04.380
OP_READ means a letter arrived.

18
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.405
And OP_WRITE means there's room to send a reply.

19
00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:09.889
Each registration is a SelectionKey.

20
00:01:09.889 --> 00:01:11.769
Think of it as the box itself.

21
00:01:11.769 --> 00:01:16.720
It knows its interest set, and its ready set, the lights that are on right now.

22
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.423
And it has an attachment: a card pinned to the box, where you keep that connection's state.

23
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:24.688
Now the clerk's day.

24
00:01:24.688 --> 00:01:27.941
select sleeps until at least one light comes on.

25
00:01:27.941 --> 00:01:31.396
Then selectedKeys hands over the list of lit boxes.

26
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.445
The clerk walks down the list.

27
00:01:33.445 --> 00:01:35.412
An accept key is a new customer.

28
00:01:35.412 --> 00:01:41.931
So the clerk accepts the channel, makes it non-blocking, and gives it a box of its own, with a fresh card.

29
00:01:41.931 --> 00:01:48.203
Any other key is a box with something to do, and serve handles it: read, write, and update the lights.

30
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:55.666
One line in that loop looks like housekeeping: it.remove, which crosses the box off the list.

31
00:01:55.666 --> 00:01:57.116
Does it really matter?

32
00:01:57.116 --> 00:02:05.886
The lesson's StaleKeyDemo sends exactly one message, six bytes, and runs the loop for 300 milliseconds, with the line and without it.

33
00:02:05.886 --> 00:02:10.040
How many times does each version treat the channel as readable?

34
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.450
With it.remove: once, six bytes.

35
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:21.198
Without it: seven times, in seven passes of the loop, and still six bytes in total.

36
00:02:21.198 --> 00:02:24.731
Each extra visit called read, and got nothing back.

37
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:26.160
Here's why.

38
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.190
The selector adds keys to the list, and never takes them out.

39
00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:31.247
That's your job.

40
00:02:31.247 --> 00:02:36.203
Leave a key in, and the next pass handles it again, with its old ready set.

41
00:02:36.203 --> 00:02:39.440
It's a list of lit boxes that nobody crosses off.

42
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:42.454
Seven visits to a box that held one letter.

43
00:02:42.454 --> 00:02:45.435
A very dedicated clerk, with no memory at all.

44
00:02:45.435 --> 00:02:49.713
For an accept key it's worse: accept again, and you get null back.

45
00:02:50.633 --> 00:02:53.485
The selector comes with a threading rule, too.

46
00:02:53.485 --> 00:02:58.691
A selector is safe to use from several threads, but its list of selected keys isn't.

47
00:02:58.691 --> 00:03:06.253
So every library does the simple thing: one thread owns a selector, runs its loop, and does all the work for its channels.

48
00:03:06.253 --> 00:03:09.476
Netty, next lesson, calls that thread an event loop.

49
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:17.846
One level down: on Linux, Selector.open gives you a wrapper around epoll, and on macOS, around kqueue.

50
00:03:17.846 --> 00:03:20.985
The JDK uses epoll in level-triggered mode.

51
00:03:20.985 --> 00:03:29.599
As long as a socket is ready, every select reports it again, like a light that stays on while there's mail in the box.

52
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:35.149
The other mode, edge-triggered, is more like a bell that rings once when a letter arrives.

53
00:03:35.149 --> 00:03:38.001
For reading, a light that stays on is forgiving.

54
00:03:38.001 --> 00:03:41.745
Leave a letter in the box, and the light is still on next time.

55
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.501
But here the post office picture misleads us.

56
00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:51.043
A socket counts as writable whenever its send buffer has room, and that's nearly always.

57
00:03:51.043 --> 00:03:55.892
So if every box asks for OP_WRITE, its room-for-a-reply light never goes out.

58
00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:02.841
The lesson's SpinDemo watched one idle connection for 300 milliseconds.

59
00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:05.975
With OP_READ alone, zero wakeups.

60
00:04:05.975 --> 00:04:08.728
With OP_WRITE added, 374,510.

61
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:17.735
That's the clerk walking to a box, finding room for a reply nobody wrote, and walking back, more than a thousand times a millisecond.

62
00:04:17.735 --> 00:04:19.918
Great for the clerk's step count.

63
00:04:19.918 --> 00:04:24.617
On a real server, it's one core at 100 percent, with no traffic at all.

64
00:04:24.617 --> 00:04:27.925
So ask for OP_WRITE only while a reply is waiting.

65
00:04:28.833 --> 00:04:37.373
Here's the clerk's day in code, in Java and in Kotlin: select, the list of lit boxes, it.remove, and the two kinds of keys.

66
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.216
So how does one clerk know which boxes are lit?

67
00:04:41.216 --> 00:04:44.752
It asks the selector, which hands over only the lit ones.

68
00:04:44.752 --> 00:04:47.917
And the selector never crosses a box off your list.

69
00:04:47.917 --> 00:04:53.004
Remove every key you handle, and ask for the write light only while a reply waits.

70
00:04:53.233 --> 00:04:54.048
Quick check.

71
00:04:54.048 --> 00:04:57.646
You forget it.remove, and a client sends one message.

72
00:04:57.646 --> 00:05:00.293
Does the server read those bytes twice?

73
00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:03.681
No.

74
00:05:03.681 --> 00:05:08.614
It visits the box again and again, but each extra read finds nothing.

75
00:05:08.614 --> 00:05:11.188
Wasted trips, not duplicate letters.
